  "overview": {
    "text": "Osteoporosis is a disease in which bone mineral density or mass falls, or bone structure changes, making bones weaker and more likely to fracture. Bone is living tissue that is continually renewed; when breakdown outpaces replacement, its inner honeycomb-like structure thins and the spaces widen. Often there are no warning symptoms until a break occurs, sometimes after a minor fall or even a cough; hip, spine, and wrist are frequent fracture sites. It can affect people of any sex or background, but risk rises with age and is higher in women after menopause. A low-dose DXA/DEXA scan measures bone minerals and helps identify osteoporosis and estimate fracture risk; osteopenia is low bone density less severe than osteoporosis.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis",
      "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/osteoporosis/osteoporosis",
      "https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/bone-density-scan/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "When bone breakdown exceeds replacement, the inner bone structure thins, its spaces grow, and the outer shell becomes thinner—weakening the bone.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/osteoporosis/osteoporosis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Osteoporosis is often symptomless until a fracture; in weakened bones, a minor fall or ordinary stress such as coughing can sometimes cause one.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis",
        "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/osteoporosis/osteoporosis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Fractures commonly affect the hip, spine, or wrist. Spinal fractures may cause severe back pain, height loss, or a hunched posture.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/osteoporosis/osteoporosis",
        "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Risk rises with age; other risk factors include low estrogen after menopause, family history, too little calcium or vitamin D, inactivity, and long-term use of some medicines.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis",
        "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/osteoporosis/osteoporosis"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A DXA/DEXA scan uses low-dose X-rays to measure bone minerals. Results can help diagnose osteoporosis and assess future fracture risk.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/bone-density-scan/"
      ]
    }
  ],
